>> title: Pastrami
>> category: Smoked & Grilled
>> tags: beef, cured, smoked, steamed, deli
>> counters: Deli
>> aka: hot pastrami, hand-cut pastrami, pastrami sandwich, deli pastrami, beef navel pastrami, pastrami on rye
>> pairs-with: deli-rye-bread, sour-dill-pickles, corned-beef, russian-dressing
>> servings: 8
>> time: 5 days 10 hr 30 min
>> slack: narrow — the brine has real edges in both directions: under five days the middle is still grey when you cut it, past seven it is salt and nothing else, and neither is fixable afterwards; fry a coin before the rub goes on, because after that another hour of soaking is no longer available
>> step.1: stir the brine
>> step.2: brine 5 days, turn daily
>> step.3: rinse, soak 2 hr, pat dry
>> step.4: press on the pepper and coriander
>> step.5: smoke 6 hr at 225°F (107°C)
>> step.6: steam 2 hr, rest 30 min, slice by hand

Stir @water{4%L}(1 gal, cold), @kosher salt{200%g}(1 cup Diamond Crystal), @dark brown sugar{100%g}(1/2 cup packed), @pink curing salt{25%g}(5 tsp; cure #1, the 6.25% nitrite kind — not table salt and not pink Himalayan), @pickling spice{4%Tbs} and @garlic{10%cloves}(crushed) until the salt and sugar have gone. The pink salt is what makes this pastrami rather than pot roast: it holds the meat red through a long low smoke and it is the only ingredient here with a dose.

Submerge @beef navel{5%lb}(2.3 kg; brisket point is the usual stand-in) in @&(~1)brine{} in a #non-reactive tub{}, weight it under the surface, and ~brine{5%days} in the fridge, turning it over once a day. Under five days the middle is still grey when you cut it; past seven it is salt and nothing else.

Rinse @&(~1)cured beef{} and ~soak{2%hr} in @cold water{4%L}(1 gal, changed once), then pat it dry. Fry a coin of it to taste before you commit — if the coin is too salty, another hour of soaking is the fix, and after the rub it is not.

Press @cracked black peppercorns{1/2%cup}(55 g, coarse), @coriander seed{1/4%cup}(25 g, toasted and cracked), @sweet paprika{1%Tbs} and @granulated garlic{1%Tbs} onto every face of @&(~1)soaked beef{} until no meat shows. Coarse, not ground: the crust is meant to be a layer you can see at the edge of each slice.

Smoke @&(~1)rubbed beef{} fat cap up in a #smoker{} over @oak or hickory wood{3%chunks} at 225°F (107°C) and ~smoke{6%hr}, until the inside reads 165°F (74°C). It is not tender yet and it is not supposed to be — the smoke is the flavour step, and the next one is the cooking step.

Steam @&(~1)smoked beef{} over @water{2%L}(boiling) in a covered pot for ~steam{2%hr}, until 203°F (95°C) and a probe slides in like into soft butter, then ~rest{30%min} and cut it across the grain by hand, a little thicker than you think. The steam is the whole difference between this and smoked brisket, and a machine slicer makes it into something else. To eat it the way the counter sells it: two inches piled on seeded rye with yellow mustard, and nothing else on the plate but a pickle.
